<%#Import the header%>
<%- include('../templates/header'); -%>
<%#/Import the header%>


<section class="fanart">
<div class="fanart-container">
  <div class="fanart-masthead">
    <h1>Fanart</h1>
    <time>Updated — Winter 2026</time>
  </div>

  <section class="fanart-hero">
    <div>
      <h2>The Best Fanart<br>in the fandom</h2>
      <p class="fanart-hero-text">All of the Harry and Hermione fanart, collected into one place, finally...</p><br>
    </div>

  </section>


  <section class="fanart-body-content">
    <div class="fanart-section-label">Artists</div>
    <div class="fanart-body-text">
      <p>In the pursuit of visual clarity, what we choose to omit becomes as crucial as what we include. This fundamental principle guides contemporary design thinking.</p>
      <p>The intentional use of empty space - what designers call negative space or white space—creates rhythm, hierarchy, and breathing room within compositions. It allows elements to exist without competing for attention, establishing a visual language that speaks through restraint rather than abundance.</p>
      <a href="/fanart/artists" class="btn btn-sm w-25 btn-primary">View Artists</a>
    </div>
    <div class="fanart-grid-image">
          <a href="/fanart/artists">
          <img class="w-100 " src="/images/fanartpage/artists.jpg" alt="Photo of artists">
          </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="fanart-quote-section">
    <blockquote>
      "Perfection is achieved not when there is nothing more to add, but when there is nothing left to take away."
    </blockquote>
    <p class="fanart-quote-attribution">— Antoine de Saint-Exupéry</p>
  </section>

  <section class="fanart-body-content">
    <div class="fanart-section-label">Safe for Work Art</div>
    <div class="fanart-body-text">
      <p>In the pursuit of visual clarity, what we choose to omit becomes as crucial as what we include. This fundamental principle guides contemporary design thinking.</p>
      <p>The intentional use of empty space - what designers call negative space or white space—creates rhythm, hierarchy, and breathing room within compositions. It allows elements to exist without competing for attention, establishing a visual language that speaks through restraint rather than abundance.</p>
      <a href="/fanart/sfw" class="btn btn-sm w-25 btn-primary">View SFW Art</a>

    </div>
    <div class="fanart-grid-image">
          <a href="/fanart/sfw">
          <img class="w-100 " src="/images/fanartpage/sfw.jpg" alt="Photo of artists">
          </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="fanart-quote-section">

  </section>

  <section class="fanart-body-content">
    <div class="fanart-section-label">Not Safe for Work Art</div>
    <div class="fanart-body-text">
      <p>In the pursuit of visual clarity, what we choose to omit becomes as crucial as what we include. This fundamental principle guides contemporary design thinking.</p>
      <p>The intentional use of empty space - what designers call negative space or white space—creates rhythm, hierarchy, and breathing room within compositions. It allows elements to exist without competing for attention, establishing a visual language that speaks through restraint rather than abundance.</p>
      <a href="/fanart/nsfw" class="btn btn-sm w-25 btn-primary">View NSFW Art</a>

    </div>
    <div class="fanart-grid-image">
          <a href="/fanart/nsfw">
          <img class="w-100 " src="/images/fanartpage/nsfw.jpg" alt="Photo of artists">
          </a>
    </div>
  </section>



  <div class="fanart-footer">
    <p>Fanart Page</p>
    <p>Harry and Hermione</p>
  </div>
</div>
</section>


